* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !pw_complex(pw)
*
*    Returns @true when pw satisfies SD's password rule, @false otherwise.
*    It sets no status and touches nothing: it is a predicate over a string.
*
*    THE RULE, and it is a CONTRACT SHARED WITH THE LINUX PORT rather than a
*    local choice - the two implementations are checked against the same
*    table, so do not "improve" it on one side only:
*
*      - at least 8 characters, no maximum;
*      - at least one lower-case letter    a-z;
*      - at least one upper-case letter    A-Z;
*      - at least one digit                0-9;
*      - at least one SYMBOL, which means any OTHER printable ASCII
*        character, 32 to 126 - AND SPACE COUNTS AS ONE;
*      - any character outside 32-126 fails outright: a tab, a control
*        character, or anything non-ASCII such as an accented letter.
*
*    WHY ASCII CODE RANGES RATHER THAN CHARACTER CLASSES.  The rule is
*    written in BASIC here and in bash on the Linux side, and a byte range
*    reads the same in both; "is this a letter" does not, once a locale is
*    involved.  The Linux agent asked for it in these terms.
*
*    WHY NON-ASCII FAILS RATHER THAN COUNTING AS A SYMBOL.  A password is
*    typed again later, on a console whose encoding nobody has promised - and
*    on this port it may also be handed to Windows, which stores it as UTF-16
*    while SD holds bytes.  A character that survives one round trip and not
*    the next is a lockout, so it is refused at the door where the refusal
*    can still be explained.
*
*    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.  It does not check a dictionary, a history, or the
*    account name - Linux's PAM stack does some of that and this deliberately
*    does not try to reproduce it.  ***AND IT IS NOT THE ONLY RULE IN FORCE
*    ON THIS PORT***: the callers that set a WINDOWS password still hand it
*    to Windows afterwards, and the machine's own policy can refuse what this
*    accepts.  SD's rule is a floor, never a ceiling.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function pw_complex(pw)
$catalog !pw_complex

   pw.len = len(pw)
   if pw.len < 8 then return @false

   got.lower = @false
   got.upper = @false
   got.digit = @false
   got.symbol = @false

*  ONE PASS, AND THE ORDER OF THE CASES IS THE RULE.  The out-of-range test
*  is first so that a byte outside 32-126 can never fall through to the
*  symbol arm and satisfy the very requirement it is supposed to fail.
   for pw.i = 1 to pw.len
      pw.c = seq(pw[pw.i, 1])
      begin case
      case pw.c < 32 or pw.c > 126
         return @false
      case pw.c >= 97 and pw.c <= 122
         got.lower = @true
      case pw.c >= 65 and pw.c <= 90
         got.upper = @true
      case pw.c >= 48 and pw.c <= 57
         got.digit = @true
      case @true
         got.symbol = @true
      end case
   next pw.i

   if not(got.lower)  then return @false
   if not(got.upper)  then return @false
   if not(got.digit)  then return @false
   if not(got.symbol) then return @false

return @true
end
